<h2>The FIR and the rival positions</h2>

<p>FIR No. 276/2026 was registered on 12 August 2026 at Police Station Maidan Garhi for an incident of 8 August 2026 — a four-day gap the defence emphasised. The sections invoked were 110 (attempt to commit culpable homicide), 126(2) (wrongful restraint), 332(c) (house-trespass) and 3(5) (common intention) of the BNS. The defence described the episode as a scuffle between the applicant's sisters-in-law within a circle of known and related persons, pointed to the medico-legal record describing the injuries as simple, and noted that the applicant's husband — a co-accused — had already secured interim anticipatory protection in his own application a week earlier. The State opposed, submitting that the FIR contained specific allegations against the applicant.</p>

<h2>The order</h2>

<p>Justice Sachin Datta granted anticipatory bail. The order rests on an assessment familiar from settled anticipatory bail doctrine: the nature of the accusation and the antecedents of the applicant, the possibility of fleeing, and whether the accusation appears aimed at humiliating or injuring the applicant through arrest. Three features did the work here.</p>

<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h3>Cooperation</h3><p>The applicant had joined the investigation from time to time — custodial interrogation had no evident purpose a jail cell would serve better than a police station interview.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Parity</h3><p>The husband, placed similarly as co-accused, already held interim protection; like cases being treated alike is a recurring thread in bail practice.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Antecedents and context</h3><p>No criminal history, simple injuries on the MLC, a four-day-old complaint and a dispute among relatives — a matrix where arrest would punish before trial rather than aid investigation.</p></div>
</div>

<p>The court recorded the State's contention of specific attribution and still found the balance favoured protection — a reminder that specific allegations in an FIR do not by themselves defeat pre-arrest bail; they are weighed with everything else. The operative direction: on arrest, release on a personal bond of Rs 25,000 with one surety in the like amount, subject to the usual conditions on travel, addresses, mobile location sharing, monthly reporting to the SHO, attendance, and strict non-contact with the complainant and witnesses. The court expressly confined its observations to the bail application, leaving the merits untouched.</p>

<h2>Reading the order as a practice template</h2>

<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Building block</th><th>What to place before the court</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Nature of the dispute</td><td>Relationship between the parties, history of civil or matrimonial friction, cross-complaints if any</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Medical record</td><td>The MLC — simple injuries materially change the complexion of offences like Section 110 BNS</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Timing</td><td>Gap between incident and FIR, and any intervening events that explain or undercut the complaint</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Conduct</td><td>Proof of joining the investigation — notices received and honoured under Section 35(3) BNSS</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Parity</td><td>Orders in co-accused applications, with the roles compared, not merely cited</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>A note on the provisions</h2>

<p>Because the incident post-dates 1 July 2024, the new codes apply throughout: the substantive offences come from the BNS, and anticipatory bail is governed by Section 482 BNSS, which carries forward the essentials of Section 438 CrPC. Delhi practice continues to allow the applicant to choose between the Court of Session and the High Court, though moving the High Court directly is ordinarily reserved for cases with special circumstances. Orders of this kind also show the modern texture of conditions — location-enabled phones and monthly SHO reporting have become standard tools by which courts reconcile liberty with investigative oversight.</p>

<div class="note"><p>This article summarises a Delhi High Court anticipatory bail order for general information. Every application turns on its own record, and observations in bail orders do not decide the merits. Nothing here is legal advice.</p></div>
